  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Ethiopians have no family surnames. Names are patronymic: a person's own given name, followed by the father's given name, and sometimes the grandfather's, so Selamawit Tesfaye is Selamawit, daughter of Tesfaye, and her brother may be Dawit Tesfaye. Women keep their own father's name when they marry. The 'family' lists below are fathers' given names, which sit where a surname would. People are addressed by their first name, with Ato (Mr), Weizero (Mrs) or Weizerit (Miss) for politeness: Ato Dawit, never Ato Tesfaye. Amharic names are often meaningful words or short sentences (Tesfaye, my hope; Selamawit, of peace); Oromo names often carry wishes or the time of birth (Chaltu, the better one; Gemechu, joy); Tigrinya names often begin with Gebre- (servant of) or Haile- (power of). Names are written here in Latin letters, the way people text them, though they are written in Ge'ez script in Ethiopia.",
